#align(center)[#text(size: 16pt, weight: "bold")[An explicit obstruction to nuclear norm-ultrapower embeddings]]
#align(center)[OpenAI]
#align(center)[September 23, 2026]

== 1 The embedding problem <sec:introduction>

Throughout, $C^(*)$-algebras are complex, and an #emph[embedding] is an injective $*$-homomorphism. We state unit preservation explicitly. For a nonzero unital $C^(*)$-algebra $B$ and a free ultrafilter $omega$ on $bb(N)$, its #emph[norm ultrapower] is

$ B^omega = ell^oo \( bb(N) \, B \) \/ I_omega \( B \) \, #h(2em) I_omega \( B \) = { \( b_n \) : lim_omega parallel b_n parallel = 0 } . $

Here $ell^oo \( bb(N) \, B \)$ is the algebra of bounded sequences, and the quotient uses the operator norm. The Cuntz algebra $cal(O)_2$ is the universal unital $C^(*)$-algebra generated by two isometries $s_1 \, s_2$ satisfying $s_1 s_1^(*) + s_2 s_2^(*) = 1$; it was introduced by Cuntz #cite(label("Cuntz1977")).

The embedding theorem of Kirchberg–Phillips #cite(label("KirchbergPhillips2000")) states that every separable unital exact $C^(*)$-algebra embeds unitally into $cal(O)_2$. Here exactness means that minimal tensoring preserves short exact sequences. Passing from $cal(O)_2$ to its norm ultrapower asks how far this universality extends beyond exact algebras. The techniques behind the embedding theorem led Kirchberg to consider this possibility, as recounted in #cite(label("GoldbringSinclair2015")) Section 1.

Kirchberg’s embedding problem asks whether every separable unital $C^(*)$-algebra admits a unital embedding into $cal(O)_2^omega$; see Goldbring–Sinclair #cite(label("GoldbringSinclair2015")) Section 3.1. We give an explicit counterexample. The unital formulation is equivalent to the formulation without unit preservation #cite(label("GoldbringSinclair2015")) Section 3.1. This question concerns norm ultrapowers of a nuclear $C^(*)$-algebra. It is distinct from the tensor-norm formulation of Kirchberg’s conjecture and from Connes’ embedding problem, as emphasized in #cite(label("GoldbringSinclair2015")) Section 1.

#thm([Theorem 1.1.])[
  #emph[Let 
$ G = bb(Z) \[ 1 \/ 2 \]^3 times.r #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] "SL"_3 \( bb(Z) \) times bb(Z) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] \, #h(2em) \( M \, k \) dot.op v = 2^k M v \, $

 and let $A = C^(*) \( G \)$ be its full group $C^(*)$-algebra. Then $A$ is separable and unital. For every nonzero unital nuclear $C^(*)$-algebra $B$ and every free ultrafilter $omega$ on $bb(N)$, there is no unital embedding 
$ A arrow.r B^omega . $

 In particular, $A$ does not embed unitally into $cal(O)_2^omega$.]
] <thm:main>

The group is countable and independent of both $B$ and $omega$; no separability assumption on $B$ is needed. The proof treats an arbitrary fixed nuclear $B$ throughout. Specializing to the nuclear algebra $cal(O)_2$ gives the negative answer to the embedding problem; for its nuclearity, see #cite(label("PaulsenZheng2016")) Section 4, pp. 80–82. Section 6 gives the consequences for existential closure, permanence of $cal(O)_2^omega$-embeddability under crossed products, and good nuclear witnesses, with the relevant notions defined there.

=== 1.1 Previous work and the new obstruction <previous-work-and-the-new-obstruction>

Recent approaches study computability and tensor norms. Fox–Goldbring–Hart #cite(label("FoxGoldbringHart2024")) derive computability-theoretic consequences of a positive answer, using the computable presentation of $cal(O)_2$. Goldbring–Sinclair give a conditional route to a negative answer through a #emph[weakly explicit minimal tensor norm]: an effective choice of universal formulas approximating the minimal tensor-norm predicates to a fixed accuracy. They leave this property of $cal(O)_2$ open #cite(label("GoldbringSinclair2025Tensor")) Definition 4.16, Theorem 4.18 and Question 4.20. Our obstruction instead comes from the representation theory of an explicit discrete group.

The group configuration already appears in the nonfiniteness arguments of Sauers #cite(label("Sauers2026NonMF")) Section 2.4 and Eckhardt #cite(label("Eckhardt2026")) Theorem 3.1 and Example 3.2. They show that the full algebra of the ascending HNN extension $G$ is nonfinite: it contains a proper isometry $v$, so $v^(*) v = 1$ but $v v^(*) eq.not 1$. Its property (T) base group $Gamma = bb(Z)^3 times.r "SL"_3 \( bb(Z) \)$ and its finite-index scalar self-embeddings are recorded in de Cornulier #cite(label("deCornulier2007")) Proposition 1.1 and its proof. Sauers and Eckhardt also construct non-MF groups: groups admitting no injective homomorphism into the unitary group of any norm matrix corona $product_j M_(d_j) \/ xor.big_j M_(d_j)$, with positive integer dimensions $d_j$. Here the product consists of bounded sequences and the direct sum is the ideal of sequences tending to zero in norm #cite(label("Eckhardt2026")) Theorem 4.3#cite(label("Sauers2026NonMF")) Sections 3–5. Eckhardt’s example is an associated wreath product. The target class here is different: nonfiniteness of $C^(*) \( G \)$ alone cannot exclude a unital embedding into $cal(O)_2^omega$, which itself contains proper isometries.

The additional ingredient is a finiteness statement for approximate finite-dimensional representation types inside a nuclear algebra (Lemma 6). Its tensor-product argument follows Wassermann’s method #cite(label("Wassermann1991")), as explained by Manuilov–Thomsen #cite(label("ManuilovThomsen2007")) Section 2.1. The statement applies to an arbitrary tuple of unitaries, even when the tuple satisfies no group relations. This flexibility is essential because coordinate lifts of an ultrapower representation are only approximately multiplicative. A dimension-independent estimate allows the representation type to vary with the coordinate. Finite-index induction then relates members of the resulting finite sets by composition with the self-embedding. These two passages turn the group configuration into the norm-ultrapower obstruction.

=== 1.2 Proof outline <proof-outline>

The subgroup $Gamma = bb(Z)^3 times.r "SL"_3 \( bb(Z) \)$ of $G$ has property (T). Conjugation by the generator $t$ of the last $bb(Z)$-factor sends $Gamma$ onto the index-eight subgroup $theta \( Gamma \)$, where $theta \( v \, M \) = \( 2 v \, M \)$. There is a finite-dimensional irreducible representation $pi_(*)$ of $Gamma$ such that $pi_(*) compose theta^j$ is inequivalent to $pi_(*)$ for every $j gt.eq 1$. Section 2 verifies these facts.

Fix a finite generating set $S$ of $Gamma$ for which property (T) gives a spectral gap. Suppose a unital embedding $A arrow.r B^omega$ exists, and choose unitary lifts $x_n \( g \) in B$ of its group unitaries. For each $n$, consider the nonzero finite-dimensional irreducible unitary representations $pi$ for which the tuple $\( x_n \( s \) ⊗ macron(pi) \( s \) \)_(s in S)$ has spectral minimum below a fixed small threshold for the sum of squared displacements from $1$. Here $macron(pi)$ is the conjugate representation. Sections 3 and 4 specify the threshold and show that the resulting set $D_n$ of representation classes is finite.

Write $pi prec rho$ when $pi$ occurs as a unitary subrepresentation of $rho$, and call $pi'$ a predecessor of $pi$ if $pi prec pi' compose theta$. Faithfulness of the embedding places $\[ pi_(*) \]$ in $D_n$ on a set belonging to $omega$. In Section 5, the spectral gap for #emph[every sequence] of finite-dimensional representations supplies, on one set belonging to $omega$, the simultaneous implication

$ \[ pi \] in D_n quad arrow.r.double quad upright("some ") \[ pi' \] in D_n upright(" satisfies ") pi prec pi' compose theta . $

Starting at $pi_(*)$ and repeatedly choosing a predecessor must give a repetition in the finite set $D_n$. Irreducibility then makes $pi_(*)$ periodic under composition with $theta$, a contradiction.

== 2 The group and a finite-dimensional representation <sec:group>

We construct a finite-dimensional representation inequivalent to each of its positive pullbacks along a fixed group endomorphism. The same group configuration appears in the nonfiniteness arguments of Sauers #cite(label("Sauers2026NonMF")) Section 2.4 and Eckhardt #cite(label("Eckhardt2026")) Theorem 3.1 and Example 3.2. De Cornulier #cite(label("deCornulier2007")) Proposition 1.1 and its proof records the property (T) affine group and its finite-index scalar self-embeddings. We use the dyadic case.

We use column vectors. In the group $G$ of Theorem 1, write elements as $\( v \, M \, k \)$, so that

$ \( v \, M \, k \) \( w \, N \, ell \) = \( v + 2^k M w \, M N \, k + ell \) . $

Set

$ Gamma = { \( v \, M \, 0 \) : v in bb(Z)^3 \, med M in "SL"_3 \( bb(Z) \) } \, #h(2em) t = \( 0 \, I \, 1 \) . $

We suppress the final coordinate when writing elements of $Gamma$, and define

$ theta : Gamma arrow.r Gamma \, #h(2em) theta \( v \, M \) = \( 2 v \, M \) \, #h(2em) Lambda = theta \( Gamma \) . $

#thm([Lemma 2.1.])[
  #emph[The group $Gamma$ is finitely generated and has property (T). The map $theta$ is injective, 
$ \[ Gamma : Lambda \] = 8 \, #h(2em) t gamma t^(- 1) = theta \( gamma \) quad \( gamma in Gamma \) . $

 There is a nonzero finite-dimensional irreducible unitary representation $pi_(*)$ of $Gamma$ that factors through reduction modulo $2$ and is nontrivial on the translation subgroup $bb(Z)^3$. In particular, 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1)")
 $ pi_(*) tilde.eq.not pi_(*) compose theta^j #h(2em) \( j gt.eq 1 \) . $ <eq:group-aperiodic>]

]
] <lem:group-data>

#proof[
  Property (T) is the case $n = 3$, $m = 1$ of #cite(label("deCornulier2007")) Proposition 1.1, with $"SL"_3 \( bb(Z) \)$ acting on one copy of its standard rank-three integral module. For finite generation, take the three standard translations and the elementary matrices $I + E_(i j)$, $i eq.not j$, where $E_(i j)$ is the matrix unit. These matrices generate $"SL"_3 \( bb(Z) \)$ by integer row reduction.
  The group law shows that $theta$ is an injective homomorphism and that conjugation by $t$ implements it. Its image is $Lambda = 2 bb(Z)^3 times.r "SL"_3 \( bb(Z) \)$. The elements
  $ r_a = \( a \, I \) \, #h(2em) a in { 0 \, 1 }^3 \, $
  are representatives of its eight left cosets: writing $v = a + 2 w$ gives $\( v \, M \) = r_a \( 2 w \, M \)$, and two such representatives give the same coset only when they have the same residue modulo $2$.
  Let $F$ be the finite image of the reduction homomorphism
  $ Gamma arrow.r \( bb(Z) \/ 2 bb(Z) \)^3 times.r "SL"_3 \( bb(Z) \/ 2 bb(Z) \) \, #h(2em) \( v \, M \) mapsto \( v med mod med 2 \, M med mod med 2 \) . $
  The translation subgroup $\( bb(Z) \/ 2 bb(Z) \)^3$ acts nontrivially in the left regular representation of $F$. Decomposing this representation into irreducibles therefore gives an irreducible constituent that is nontrivial on translations. Its pullback to $Gamma$ is the required $pi_(*)$. Indeed, $pi_(*)$ factors through reduction modulo $2$, whereas $theta^j \( v \, I \) = \( 2^j v \, I \)$. Thus $pi_(*) compose theta^j$ is trivial on all translations for every $j gt.eq 1$, and $pi_(*)$ is not. This proves (1). ◻
]

The group $G$ is countable, so its full group $C^(*)$-algebra $C^(*) \( G \)$ is separable and unital. Every unitary representation of $G$ extends to $C^(*) \( G \)$. We will use this universal property after inducing $pi_(*)$ from $Gamma$ to $G$.

== 3 A spectral test for representation matching <sec:spectral>

We now introduce the spectral quantity used to form the finite sets in the proof outline. The discussion applies to any finitely generated discrete group $Gamma$ with property (T). Choose a nonempty finite generating set $S$ containing a Kazhdan set. By property (T), after decreasing the constant if necessary, there is $0 < kappa lt.eq 4 \| S \|$ such that every unitary representation $sigma$ with no nonzero invariant vector satisfies

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(2)")
 $ sum_(s in S) parallel sigma \( s \) xi - xi parallel^2 gt.eq kappa parallel xi parallel^2 . $ <eq:kazhdan-gap>]

For a tuple $upright(bold(z)) = \( z_s \)_(s in S)$ of unitaries in a nonzero unital $C^(*)$-algebra $C$, set

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3)")
 $ L \( upright(bold(z)) \) = sum_(s in S) \( z_s - 1 \)^(*) \( z_s - 1 \) \, #h(2em) R = 4 \| S \| . $ <eq:laplacian>]

Thus $0 lt.eq L \( upright(bold(z)) \) lt.eq R 1$, without any group relations on $upright(bold(z))$.

#thm([Lemma 3.1.])[
  #emph[If $z_s = sigma \( s \)$ for a unitary representation $sigma : Gamma arrow.r "U" \( C \)$ in a nonzero unital $C^(*)$-algebra, then 
$ "spec" L \( upright(bold(z)) \) subset.eq { 0 } union \[ kappa \, R \] . $

]
] <lem:spectral-gap>

#proof[
  Represent $C$ faithfully and unitally on a Hilbert space. The invariant vectors for $sigma$ and their orthogonal complement are reducing subspaces. On the former $L$ vanishes; on the latter (2) gives $L gt.eq kappa 1$. A faithful unital representation preserves spectra, proving the claim. ◻
]

Every finite-dimensional representation below acts on a nonzero Hilbert space. If $pi : Gamma arrow.r "U" \( V_pi \)$ is such a representation, its conjugate $macron(pi)$ acts on the conjugate Hilbert space $macron(V)_pi$ by $macron(pi) \( g \) macron(v) = overline(pi \( g \) v)$. Define

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4)")
 $ d \( upright(bold(z)) \, pi \) = min "spec" L #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] \( z_s ⊗ macron(pi) \( s \) \)_(s in S) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] \, $ <eq:matching-distance>]

where the spectrum is taken in $C ⊗ cal(B) \( macron(V)_pi \)$. Matrix algebras have a unique $C^(*)$-tensor norm. On any faithful unital Hilbert-space realization $C subset.eq cal(B) \( H \)$, the number in (4) equals

$ inf_(xi in H ⊗ macron(V)_pi\
parallel xi parallel = 1) sum_(s in S) parallel \( z_s ⊗ macron(pi) \( s \) \) xi - xi parallel^2 . $

In such a faithful realization, if $z_s = rho \( s \)$ comes from a unitary representation and $pi$ is irreducible, then $d \( upright(bold(z)) \, pi \) = 0$ exactly when $pi$ occurs in $rho$, and otherwise $d \( upright(bold(z)) \, pi \) gt.eq kappa$. Indeed, invariant vectors of $rho ⊗ macron(pi)$ correspond to intertwiners from $V_pi$ to $H$, and the spectral gap separates their orthogonal complement from zero.

The number $d \( upright(bold(z)) \, pi \)$ is unchanged by unitary equivalence of $pi$. For a finite direct sum, the same formula gives

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5)")
 $ d \( upright(bold(z)) \, pi_1 xor dots.h.c xor pi_r \) = min_(1 lt.eq i lt.eq r) d \( upright(bold(z)) \, pi_i \) . $ <eq:block-minimum>]

Fix once and for all

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(6)")
 $ b = frac(kappa^2, 4 \| S \|) \, #h(2em) 0 < a < min { kappa \, b } . $ <eq:thresholds>]

The next section proves that, for a tuple in a nuclear algebra, only finitely many irreducible $pi$ can satisfy $d \( upright(bold(z)) \, pi \) < a$. No group relations on that tuple will be needed.

== 4 Finiteness of approximate representation matches <sec:nuclear>

We prove that, for a fixed unitary tuple $upright(bold(u))$ in a nuclear algebra, only finitely many finite-dimensional irreducible representation classes satisfy $d \( upright(bold(u)) \, pi \) < a$. The tuple need not satisfy any group relations. This distinction will allow us to apply the result to coordinate lifts from a norm ultrapower.

We retain the notation and constants of Section 3. For $C^(*)$-algebras $B$ and $C$, the minimal tensor product $B ⊗_min C$ is realized by faithful representations on separate Hilbert spaces; the maximal tensor product $B ⊗_max C$ is characterized by pairs of commuting representations on one Hilbert space. We use the tensor-norm characterization of nuclearity developed by Takesaki and Lance #cite(label("Takesaki1964")) #cite(label("Lance1973")): $B$ is nuclear if these two tensor norms agree for every $C$. We first obtain a lower bound when the second tensor factor has no finite-dimensional subrepresentations. The passage from a vector to its reduced density operator removes the arbitrary unitaries in the first factor.

#thm([Lemma 4.1.])[
  (Square-root trace inequality).  #emph[For positive Hilbert–Schmidt operators $X \, Y$ on any Hilbert space, 
$ parallel X - Y parallel_2^2 lt.eq parallel X^2 - Y^2 parallel_1 . $

 Here $parallel dot.op parallel_1$ and $parallel dot.op parallel_2$ denote the trace and Hilbert–Schmidt norms, respectively.]
] <lem:square-root-trace>

#proof[
  This is the square-root trace inequality of Powers–Størmer #cite(label("PowersStormer1970")) Lemma 4.1; we include the short argument needed here. Put $F = X - Y$, and let $J = "sgn" \( F \)$, with value zero on $ker F$. Since $X^2 - Y^2 = F X + Y F$ and $J F = F J = \| F \|$,
  $ parallel X^2 - Y^2 parallel_1 & gt.eq "Tr" #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] J \( X^2 - Y^2 \) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)]\
 & = "Tr" #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] \| F \| \( X + Y \) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)]\
 & gt.eq "Tr" \( F_(+) F \) + "Tr" \( F_(-) \( - F \) \) = "Tr" \( F^2 \) . $
  For the last inequality, use $X + Y - F = 2 Y gt.eq 0$ and $X + Y + F = 2 X gt.eq 0$, and pair these inequalities with $F_(+)$ and $F_(-)$, respectively.
  All these traces are well defined in infinite dimension. Products of two Hilbert–Schmidt operators are trace class; their traces are cyclic, by finite-rank approximation and $parallel A B parallel_1 lt.eq parallel A parallel_2 parallel B parallel_2$. The trace of a product of two positive Hilbert–Schmidt operators is nonnegative, as is seen by diagonalizing one factor. These observations also justify the cyclic rearrangement with the bounded operator $J$. The operators involved vanish on the orthogonal complement of a separable subspace, so no separability hypothesis on the ambient Hilbert space is needed. ◻
]

Bekka #cite(label("Bekka1990")) proved that if a unitary representation of a property (T) group on a Hilbert space $K$ admits a state on $cal(B) \( K \)$ invariant under conjugation by the representation, then it has a nonzero finite-dimensional subrepresentation; see also #cite(label("Valette1997")) proof of Proposition 2. The next lemma gives the explicit spectral bound needed here for an arbitrary unitary tuple in the first tensor factor.

#thm([Lemma 4.2.])[
  (Uniform spectral bound).  #emph[Let $Gamma \, S \, kappa$ satisfy (2). Let $sigma : Gamma arrow.r cal(U) \( K \)$ be a unitary representation with no nonzero finite-dimensional subrepresentation. For any Hilbert space $E$ and any unitaries $\( U_s \)_(s in S)$ on $E$, 
$ L #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] \( U_s ⊗ sigma \( s \) \)_(s in S) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] gt.eq b 1 \, #h(2em) b = frac(kappa^2, 4 \| S \|) . $

 No relations among the $U_s$ are required.]
] <lem:weak-mixing-bound>

#proof[
  Conjugation by $sigma$ is a unitary representation on the Hilbert space $cal(S)_2 \( K \)$ of Hilbert–Schmidt operators. It has no nonzero invariant vector: if $A eq.not 0$ commutes with every $sigma \( g \)$, then so does the nonzero positive compact operator $\| A \|$. The eigenspace of any nonzero eigenvalue of $\| A \|$ is a nonzero finite-dimensional $sigma$-invariant subspace.
  Use inner products linear in the first variable. Fix a unit vector $xi in E ⊗ K$. Its reduced density operator $T$ on $K$ is characterized by
  $ "Tr" \( T C \) = chevron.l \( 1 ⊗ C \) xi \, xi chevron.r #h(2em) \( C in cal(B) \( K \) \) . $
  Concretely, write $xi = sum_i e_i ⊗ v_i$ with $\( e_i \)$ orthonormal and $sum_i parallel v_i parallel^2 = 1$. Then $T = sum_i \| v_i chevron.r chevron.l v_i \|$, with convergence in trace norm; in particular, $T gt.eq 0$ and $"Tr" \( T \) = 1$. Such an expansion uses only countably many vectors, even if the Hilbert spaces are nonseparable.
  For $s in S$, put $xi_s = \( U_s ⊗ sigma \( s \) \) xi$ and $delta_s = parallel xi_s - xi parallel$. The reduced density operator of $xi_s$ is $T_s = sigma \( s \) T sigma \( s \)^(*)$. Testing the two vector states against $1 ⊗ C$, for $parallel C parallel lt.eq 1$, gives
  $ parallel T_s - T parallel_1 lt.eq 2 delta_s . $
  Lemma 4 therefore gives
  $ parallel sigma \( s \) T^(1 \/ 2) sigma \( s \)^(*) - T^(1 \/ 2) parallel_2^2 lt.eq parallel T_s - T parallel_1 lt.eq 2 delta_s . $
  Since $parallel T^(1 \/ 2) parallel_2 = 1$, applying (2) to the conjugation representation and then Cauchy–Schwarz yields
  $ kappa lt.eq 2 sum_(s in S) delta_s lt.eq 2 sqrt(\| S \|) (sum_(s in S) delta_s^2)^(1 \/ 2) . $
  Thus $sum_s delta_s^2 gt.eq b$. Taking all unit vectors $xi$ proves the operator inequality; when $E ⊗ K = { 0 }$ it is vacuous. ◻
]

The following quotient argument uses Wassermann’s property (T) method #cite(label("Wassermann1991")); see the exposition in #cite(label("ManuilovThomsen2007")) Section 2.1. The preceding uniform bound allows us to use an arbitrary unitary tuple in the first factor. Nuclearity will transfer the bound to the coordinate quotient and contradict the assumed small spectral minima.

#thm([Lemma 4.3.])[
  (Nuclear finiteness).  #emph[Let $Gamma \, S \, kappa \, R \, b \, a$ be as above. For every nonzero unital nuclear $C^(*)$-algebra $B$ and every unitary $S$-tuple $upright(bold(u))$ in $B$, the set 
$ D \( upright(bold(u)) \) = { \[ pi \] : d \( upright(bold(u)) \, pi \) < a } $

 is finite, where $pi$ ranges over the nonzero finite-dimensional irreducible unitary representations of $Gamma$ and $\[ pi \]$ denotes its unitary equivalence class. No group relations on $upright(bold(u))$ are assumed.]
] <lem:nuclear-finiteness>

#proof[
  Suppose that $\( pi_j \)_(j gt.eq 1)$ are pairwise inequivalent representations with $\[ pi_j \] in D \( upright(bold(u)) \)$. Write $M_j = cal(B) \( macron(V)_(pi_j) \)$, and form
  $ Q = frac(product_(j gt.eq 1) M_j, xor.big_(j gt.eq 1) M_j) \, #h(2em) q \( g \) = \[ \( macron(pi)_j \( g \) \)_j \] quad \( g in Gamma \) . $
  The product consists of bounded sequences, and the direct sum is the ideal of sequences whose norms tend to zero in the ordinary sense. In particular, this auxiliary quotient does not involve an ultrafilter.
  Choose a faithful unital representation $eta : Q arrow.r cal(B) \( K \)$, and put $sigma = eta compose q$. We first show that $sigma$ has no nonzero finite-dimensional subrepresentations. Fix a finite-dimensional irreducible representation $tau$ of $Gamma$. Under the identification
  $ macron(V)_(pi_j) ⊗ macron(V)_tau tilde.eq cal(S)_2 \( V_tau \, macron(V)_(pi_j) \) \, $
  invariant vectors for $macron(pi)_j ⊗ macron(tau)$ correspond to intertwiners from $tau$ to $macron(pi)_j$. By irreducibility and inequivalence, such an intertwiner can exist for at most one $j$. Consequently,
  $ L #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] \( macron(pi)_j \( s \) ⊗ macron(tau) \( s \) \)_(s in S) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] gt.eq kappa 1 $
  outside at most one coordinate. Fixed-size matrix amplification commutes with this quotient, entry by entry:
  $ Q ⊗ cal(B) \( macron(V)_tau \) tilde.eq frac(product_j \( M_j ⊗ cal(B) \( macron(V)_tau \) \), xor.big_j \( M_j ⊗ cal(B) \( macron(V)_tau \) \)) . $
  The exceptional coordinate disappears, and hence
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7)")
   $ L #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] \( q \( s \) ⊗ macron(tau) \( s \) \)_(s in S) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] gt.eq kappa 1 . $ <eq:quotient-excludes-finite-parts>]
  If $sigma$ contained $tau$, then $sigma ⊗ macron(tau)$ would have an invariant vector, contradicting (7). Every nonzero finite-dimensional unitary representation contains an irreducible subrepresentation, so $sigma$ has none.
  Represent $B$ faithfully and unitally on a Hilbert space $E$. By Lemma 5,
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(8)")
   $ L #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] \( u_s ⊗ q \( s \) \)_(s in S) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] gt.eq b 1 quad upright("in ") B ⊗_min Q . $ <eq:spatial-lower-bound>]
  We now use nuclearity to pass this inequality to the coordinate quotient. Let
  $ C = frac(product_j \( B ⊗ M_j \), xor.big_j \( B ⊗ M_j \)) . $
  There are commuting unital $*$-homomorphisms
  $ alpha : B arrow.r C \, quad alpha \( x \) = \[ \( x ⊗ 1_(M_j) \)_j \] \, #h(2em) beta : Q arrow.r C \, quad beta \( \[ \( y_j \)_j \] \) = \[ \( 1_B ⊗ y_j \)_j \] . $
  The map $beta$ is well defined because $parallel 1_B ⊗ y_j parallel = parallel y_j parallel$. The universal property of the maximal tensor product gives a unital $*$-homomorphism
  $ Psi : B ⊗_max Q arrow.r C \, #h(2em) Psi \( x ⊗ q \) = alpha \( x \) beta \( q \) . $
  Since $B$ is nuclear, $B ⊗_min Q = B ⊗_max Q$, so (8) passes through $Psi$.
  Put $L_j = L \( \( u_s ⊗ macron(pi)_j \( s \) \)_(s in S) \)$. We have proved $\[ \( L_j \)_j \] gt.eq b 1$ in $C$. Continuous functional calculus for $f \( t \) = \( b - t \)_(+)$ now gives
  $ \[ \( f \( L_j \) \)_j \] = f \( \[ \( L_j \)_j \] \) = 0 \, #h(2em) parallel f \( L_j \) parallel = \( b - d \( upright(bold(u)) \, pi_j \) \)_(+) arrow.r 0 . $
  Thus $liminf_j d \( upright(bold(u)) \, pi_j \) gt.eq b$, whereas every term is less than $a < b$. This contradiction proves finiteness. ◻
]

The only use of nuclearity was to identify the minimal and maximal tensor products before applying $Psi$. In particular, the proof does not assume an identification of the minimal tensor product with the coordinate quotient, and it imposes no bound on the dimensions of the $pi_j$.

== 5 From an ultrapower embedding to a finite contradiction <sec:ultrapower-contradiction>

To prove Theorem 1, fix a nonzero unital nuclear $C^(*)$-algebra $B$ and a free ultrafilter $omega$ on $bb(N)$. A set of indices is called $omega$-large if it belongs to $omega$. Suppose, towards a contradiction, that there is a unital injective $*$-homomorphism

$ Phi : C^(*) \( G \) arrow.r B^omega . $

We will associate a finite set of irreducible representations of $Gamma$ to each coordinate of $Phi$. Conjugation by $t$ will force these sets to contain predecessors under restriction by $theta$. The resulting finite chains contradict the choice of $pi_(*)$.

=== 5.1 Unitary lifts and a gap for varying matrix dimensions <unitary-lifts-and-a-gap-for-varying-matrix-dimensions>

Write $u_g$ for the canonical unitary of $g in G$ in $C^(*) \( G \)$. Each unitary in $B^omega$ has a representative consisting of unitaries in $B$. Indeed, if $\( y_n \)$ represents a unitary, then

$ lim_omega parallel y_n^(*) y_n - 1 parallel = lim_omega parallel y_n y_n^(*) - 1 parallel = 0 . $

On an $omega$-large set both norms are less than $1 \/ 2$, so $y_n$ is invertible and $y_n \( y_n^(*) y_n \)^(- 1 \/ 2)$ is unitary. The difference between this unitary and $y_n$ tends to zero along $omega$, by continuous functional calculus. Set the representative equal to $1$ outside this set.

Choose such representatives $x_n \( g \) in cal(U) \( B \)$ of $Phi \( u_g \)$ for every $g in G$, with $x_n \( e \) = 1$. For each fixed pair $g \, h in G$,

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(9)")
 $ lim_omega parallel x_n \( g \) x_n \( h \) - x_n \( g h \) parallel = 0 . $ <eq:lift-defects>]

These choices impose no group relations at any individual coordinate. All uses of (9) below involve finitely many fixed group elements; no intersection over all pairs is needed. Put $upright(bold(x))_n = \( x_n \( s \) \)_(s in S)$. Among the unitary equivalence classes of nonzero finite-dimensional irreducible representations of $Gamma$, define

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(10)")
 $ D_n = { \[ pi \] : d \( upright(bold(x))_n \, pi \) < a } . $ <eq:coordinate-D>]

Here brackets denote unitary equivalence classes. Each $D_n$ is finite by Lemma 6, which applies to arbitrary unitary $S$-tuples.

The next observation provides uniform control even when the dimensions of the representations increase with $n$.

#thm([Lemma 5.1.])[
  #emph[For every sequence $\( pi_n \)$ of nonzero finite-dimensional unitary representations of $Gamma$, with no bound on their dimensions, 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(11)")
 $ lim_omega d \( upright(bold(x))_n \, pi_n \) in { 0 } union \[ kappa \, R \] . $ <eq:varying-gap>]

 In particular, if ${ n : d \( upright(bold(x))_n \, pi_n \) < a } in omega$, then this limit is zero.]
] <lem:varying-gap>

#proof[
  Let $C_n = B ⊗ cal(B) \( overline(V_(pi_n)) \)$, and form the norm ultraproduct
  $ C_omega = product_n C_n #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\/] { \( c_n \) : lim_omega parallel c_n parallel = 0 } . $
  The product consists of bounded sequences. For every such sequence,
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(12)")
   $ parallel \[ \( c_n \) \] parallel = lim_omega parallel c_n parallel . $ <eq:ultraproduct-norm>]
  For the lower bound, adding an $omega$-null sequence does not change the ultralimit of the norms. For the upper bound, if this ultralimit is $c$, replace $c_n$ by $0$ outside ${ n : parallel c_n parallel < c + epsilon } in omega$ and then let $epsilon arrow.b 0$.
  Equation (9) shows that
  $ g mapsto \[ \( x_n \( g \) ⊗ overline(pi_n) \( g \) \)_n \] \, #h(2em) g in Gamma \, $
  is a genuine unitary representation in the nonzero unital algebra $C_omega$. By Lemma 3, its Laplacian has spectrum in ${ 0 } union \[ kappa \, R \]$. Write
  $ P_n = L #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] \( x_n \( s \) ⊗ overline(pi_n) \( s \) \)_(s in S) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] . $
  Since $0 lt.eq P_n lt.eq R 1$, equation (12) gives
  $ min "spec" \[ \( P_n \) \] & = R - parallel R 1 - \[ \( P_n \) \] parallel\
 & = lim_omega #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] R - parallel R 1 - P_n parallel #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] = lim_omega d \( upright(bold(x))_n \, pi_n \) . $
  This proves (11). In the final assertion the limit is at most $a < kappa$, and hence must be zero. ◻
]

=== 5.2 The initial representation occurs <the-initial-representation-occurs>

We next use injectivity of $Phi$ to ensure that $\[ pi_(*) \] in D_n$ for an $omega$-large set of indices.

#thm([Lemma 5.2.])[
  #emph[We have $lim_omega d \( upright(bold(x))_n \, pi_(*) \) = 0$, and consequently ${ n : \[ pi_(*) \] in D_n } in omega$.]
] <lem:initial-match>

#proof[
  Induce $pi_(*)$ from $Gamma$ to $G$ on the Hilbert direct sum of copies of $V_(pi_(*))$ indexed by the left cosets $G \/ Gamma$. Explicitly, choose representatives $q_i$, including the identity: when $g q_i = q_j gamma$ with $gamma in Gamma$, the action sends coordinate $i$ to coordinate $j$ by $pi_(*) \( gamma \)$. This defines a unitary representation. The identity coordinate is invariant under $Gamma$ and carries $pi_(*)$. Thus the restriction of this representation of $G$ contains $pi_(*)$. Tensoring with $overline(pi_(*))$ gives a nonzero $Gamma$-invariant vector: on this coordinate it is $sum_j e_j ⊗ overline(e_j)$ for an orthonormal basis $\( e_j \)$ of $V_(pi_(*))$.
  Every unitary representation of $G$ gives a representation of the full group algebra $C^(*) \( G \)$. The preceding invariant vector shows that
  $ min "spec" L #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] \( u_s ⊗ overline(pi_(*)) \( s \) \)_(s in S) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] = 0 . $
  Indeed, an invertible positive element could not acquire a kernel under a unital representation. The matrix amplification of $Phi$ is injective and preserves spectra, so the corresponding spectral minimum in $B^omega ⊗ cal(B) \( overline(V_(pi_(*))) \)$ is also zero.
  For each fixed positive integer $d$ there is the canonical identification
  $ M_d \( B^omega \) tilde.equiv \( M_d \( B \) \)^omega . $
  To check its kernel, a sequence of $d$-by-$d$ matrices tends to zero in norm along $omega$ precisely when each of its finitely many entries does. Entrywise lifts give surjectivity. Applying this identification with $d = dim pi_(*)$ and the calculation of spectral minima in Lemma 7 proves the assertion. This fixed matrix amplification is distinct from the varying algebras used in that lemma. ◻
]

=== 5.3 A predecessor for every representation at one coordinate <a-predecessor-for-every-representation-at-one-coordinate>

For representations $alpha$ and $beta$ of the same group, write $alpha prec beta$ when $alpha$ is unitarily equivalent to a subrepresentation of $beta$. The required quantifier order is the following: on one $omega$-large set, every member of $D_n$ has a predecessor in that same finite set.

#thm([Lemma 5.3.])[
  #emph[The set 
$ { n : \( forall \[ pi \] in D_n \) \( exists \[ pi' \] in D_n \) quad pi prec pi' compose theta } $

 belongs to $omega$.]
] <lem:uniform-predecessor>

#proof[
  Suppose otherwise. On an $omega$-large set $F$, choose an irreducible $pi_n$ with $\[ pi_n \] in D_n$ for which no such predecessor exists. Outside $F$, choose the trivial representation. By Lemma 7,
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(13)")
   $ lim_omega d \( upright(bold(x))_n \, pi_n \) = 0 . $ <eq:chosen-zero>]
  Set $alpha_n = pi_n compose theta^(- 1)$, a representation of $Lambda$, and let $rho_n = "Ind"_Lambda^Gamma alpha_n$. We will prove that $lim_omega d \( upright(bold(x))_n \, rho_n \) = 0$ and that every irreducible constituent $pi'$ of $rho_n$ satisfies $pi_n prec pi' compose theta$. The block-minimum identity (5) will then select a constituent belonging to $D_n$ on an $omega$-large set, and the second assertion will make that constituent a predecessor of $pi_n$.
  #emph[Transport to $Lambda$.] Represent $B$ faithfully and unitally on a Hilbert space $cal(H)$, and use the same notation for its unitaries on $cal(H)$. Choose unit vectors $xi_n in cal(H) ⊗ overline(V_(pi_n))$ such that
  $ sum_(s in S) parallel \( x_n \( s \) ⊗ overline(pi_n) \( s \) \) xi_n - xi_n parallel^2 < d \( upright(bold(x))_n \, pi_n \) + 1 \/ n . $
  Because $omega$ is free, $1 \/ n$ tends to zero along $omega$. Thus
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(14)")
   $ lim_omega parallel \( x_n \( g \) ⊗ overline(pi_n) \( g \) \) xi_n - xi_n parallel = 0 #h(2em) \( g in Gamma \) . $ <eq:fixed-word-invariance>]
  Here is the fixed-word justification. Put $U_n \( g \) = x_n \( g \) ⊗ overline(pi_n) \( g \)$. For each $s in S$, $x_n \( s^(- 1) \) - x_n \( s \)^(*)$ tends to zero by (9); a unitary and its inverse have the same displacement norm on a vector. For a fixed word $g = s_1^(epsilon_1) dots.h.c s_k^(epsilon_k)$, repeated use of (9) replaces $U_n \( g \)$ by the corresponding product of $U_n \( s_j \)^(epsilon_j)$ with an error tending to zero. The displacement of that product is at most the sum of the $k$ displacements of its factors. Both the word and $k$ are fixed while $n$ varies.
  Put $eta_n = \( x_n \( t \) ⊗ 1 \) xi_n$. These are unit vectors. For $h in Lambda$ put $g = theta^(- 1) \( h \)$. The identity $h t = t g$ and (9) give
  $ lim_omega parallel x_n \( h \) x_n \( t \) - x_n \( t \) x_n \( g \) parallel = 0 . $
  Together with (14), this proves
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(15)")
   $ lim_omega parallel \( x_n \( h \) ⊗ overline(alpha_n) \( h \) \) eta_n - eta_n parallel = 0 #h(2em) \( h in Lambda \) . $ <eq:Lambda-invariance>]
  #emph[Induction to $Gamma$.] We transfer these vectors from $Lambda$ to $Gamma$ by a fixed eight-coordinate induction. Use the left coset representatives $r_1 = e \, r_2 \, dots.h \, r_8$ of $Gamma \/ Lambda$ given by the translations in ${ 0 \, 1 }^3$. For $g in Gamma$ and $1 lt.eq i lt.eq 8$, define $j \( g \, i \)$ and $h \( g \, i \) in Lambda$ uniquely by
  $ g r_i = r_(j \( g \, i \)) h \( g \, i \) . $
  The induced representation $rho_n$ acts on $xor.big_(i = 1)^8 V_(pi_n)$: $rho_n \( g \)$ sends coordinate $i$ to coordinate $j \( g \, i \)$ by $alpha_n \( h \( g \, i \) \)$. The relation
  $ h \( g_1 g_2 \, i \) = h \( g_1 \, j \( g_2 \, i \) \) h \( g_2 \, i \) $
  verifies the representation law. Permutation of the coordinates and unitarity of $alpha_n$ verify unitarity. Its conjugate has the same coordinate rule with $overline(alpha_n)$ in place of $alpha_n$.
  Define $zeta_n in cal(H) ⊗ overline(V_(rho_n))$ by
  $ \( zeta_n \)_i = 8^(- 1 \/ 2) \( x_n \( r_i \) ⊗ 1 \) eta_n . $
  The eight summands are orthogonal and each has norm $8^(- 1 \/ 2)$, so $parallel zeta_n parallel = 1$. Fix $s in S$ and $i$, and write $j = j \( s \, i \)$ and $h = h \( s \, i \)$. The component sent from $i$ to $j$ by $x_n \( s \) ⊗ overline(rho_n) \( s \)$ differs from $\( zeta_n \)_j$ by a vector whose norm is at most
  $ 8^(- 1 \/ 2) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] & parallel x_n \( s \) x_n \( r_i \) - x_n \( r_j \) x_n \( h \) parallel\
 & + parallel \( x_n \( h \) ⊗ overline(alpha_n) \( h \) \) eta_n - eta_n parallel #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] . $
  The first term tends to zero by (9) and $s r_i = r_j h$; the second does so by (15). There are only $8 \| S \|$ fixed choices involved. Hence
  $ lim_omega sum_(s in S) parallel \( x_n \( s \) ⊗ overline(rho_n) \( s \) \) zeta_n - zeta_n parallel^2 = 0 . $
  Thus $lim_omega d \( upright(bold(x))_n \, rho_n \) = 0$, and in particular $d \( upright(bold(x))_n \, rho_n \) < a$ on an $omega$-large set. All error bounds are independent of $dim pi_n$.
  #emph[Selecting a predecessor.] Decompose the finite-dimensional unitary representation $rho_n$ into irreducible summands. The corresponding Laplacian is a direct sum, so its spectral minimum is the minimum of the spectral minima of these summands. Consequently, on an $omega$-large set, some irreducible constituent $pi'_n$ of $rho_n$ has $\[ pi'_n \] in D_n$.
  Every irreducible constituent $pi'$ of $rho_n$, including the one just obtained, satisfies $pi_n prec pi' compose theta$. This is the finite-index form of Frobenius reciprocity. To verify it for every constituent, let $W$ be its invariant subspace and let $P_1 : W arrow.r V_(pi_n)$ be projection onto the identity coordinate. This map is $Lambda$-equivariant for the target representation $alpha_n$. It is nonzero: if $w in W$ has a nonzero $i$th coordinate, then
  $ P_1 rho_n \( r_i^(- 1) \) w = w_i eq.not 0 . $
  Since $alpha_n$ is irreducible, $P_1 \|_W$ is surjective. Its adjoint is an injective $Lambda$-intertwiner; after normalization by a positive scalar it is an isometric intertwiner, because $\( P_1 \|_W \) \( P_1 \|_W \)^(*)$ commutes with the irreducible $alpha_n$. It follows that $alpha_n prec pi' \|_Lambda$; composing with $theta : Gamma arrow.r Lambda$ gives the asserted containment.
  Intersect the $omega$-large set on which such a $\[ pi'_n \]$ belongs to $D_n$ with $F$. This contradicts the choice of $pi_n$ and proves the lemma. ◻
]

=== 5.4 Finite chains force periodicity <finite-chains-force-periodicity>

The analytic argument is complete. We finish with the elementary consequence of finiteness that makes the predecessor property incompatible with $pi_(*)$. Consider a chain of finite-dimensional irreducible unitary representations $tau_0 = pi \, tau_1 \, dots.h$ of $Gamma$ whose classes lie in a finite set $D$ and satisfy $tau_i prec tau_(i + 1) compose theta$. Finiteness gives indices $l gt.eq 0$ and $p gt.eq 1$ with $tau_l tilde.eq tau_(l + p)$. Figure 1 separates the initial segment of this chain from the repeated segment. The key point is that periodicity at the repeated class forces every pullback of that representation to remain irreducible, so that periodicity reaches the starting class.

