  bezier((3.7, 0.2834), (7.4, 0.2891), (4.528, 1.465), (6.572, 1.471), stroke: (thickness: 0.4pt, miter-limit: 10, dash: "dashed"))
  content((5.55, 1.173), [#text(size: 9pt)[same equivalence class]], anchor: "south", padding: 0.1176cm)
  content((1.7, -0.65), [#text(size: 9pt)[initial segment]])
  content((5.55, -0.65), [#text(size: 9pt)[$p$ predecessor steps]])
})],
  caption: [Figure 1: A chain inside one finite set $D$. An arrow from $\[ tau_i \]$ to $\[ tau_(i + 1) \]$ means $tau_i prec tau_(i + 1) compose theta$. Repetition gives $tau_l tilde.eq tau_l compose theta^p$ by equality of dimensions. The proof then shows that each pullback of $tau_l$ is irreducible, allowing this periodicity to pass back to $pi$. The initial segment can be empty. ],
) <fig:finite-chain>

#thm([Lemma 5.4.])[
  #emph[Let $theta : Gamma arrow.r Gamma$ be a homomorphism, and let $D$ be a finite set of equivalence classes of nonzero finite-dimensional irreducible unitary representations of $Gamma$. Suppose that every $\[ pi \] in D$ has $\[ pi' \] in D$ with $pi prec pi' compose theta$. Then for every $\[ pi \] in D$ there is an integer $p gt.eq 1$ such that $pi tilde.eq pi compose theta^p$.]
] <lem:finite-predecessor-cycle>

#proof[
  Starting from $tau_0 = pi$, choose a chain in $D$ with $tau_i prec tau_(i + 1) compose theta$. Finiteness gives $tau_l tilde.eq tau_(l + p)$ for some $l gt.eq 0$ and $p gt.eq 1$. Composing the inclusions along this segment yields
  $ tau_l prec tau_(l + p) compose theta^p tilde.eq tau_l compose theta^p . $
  The first and last representations have the same dimension, so
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(16)")
   $ tau_l tilde.eq tau_l compose theta^p . $ <eq:cycle-periodicity>]
  It follows by iteration that $tau_l compose theta^(k p) tilde.eq tau_l$ for every integer $k gt.eq 0$.
  For every $j gt.eq 0$, the representation $tau_l compose theta^j$ is irreducible. Indeed, if it had a nonzero proper invariant subspace, that subspace would remain invariant for $tau_l compose theta^(k p)$ whenever $k p gt.eq j$, because $theta^(k p) \( Gamma \) subset.eq theta^j \( Gamma \)$. This contradicts the irreducibility of $tau_l compose theta^(k p) tilde.eq tau_l$. The initial segment of the chain gives $pi prec tau_l compose theta^l$. Its right-hand side is irreducible, so $pi tilde.eq tau_l compose theta^l$. Composing (16) with $theta^l$ therefore gives
  $ pi compose theta^p tilde.eq tau_l compose theta^(l + p) tilde.eq tau_l compose theta^l tilde.eq pi . $
  ◻
]

Choose one index $n$ in the intersection of the $omega$-large sets from Lemmas 8 and 9. Apply Lemma 10 to $D_n$ and $pi_(*)$. It gives $pi_(*) tilde.eq pi_(*) compose theta^p$ for some $p gt.eq 1$. But $pi_(*)$ acts nontrivially on the translations $bb(Z)^3$, whereas $pi_(*) compose theta^p$ acts trivially on them: $theta^p$ multiplies these translations by $2^p$, and $pi_(*)$ factors through reduction modulo $2$. This is the required contradiction.

The argument used only that $B$ is nonzero, unital and nuclear, and that $omega$ is free. The group $G$ and the algebra $C^(*) \( G \)$ are independent of these choices. Thus the same algebra has no unital embedding into $B^omega$ for any such $B$ and any free $omega$. Taking $B = cal(O)_2$ proves the stated negative answer to Kirchberg’s embedding problem.

== 6 Consequences for existential closure and nuclear approximation <sec:consequences>

We spell out the model-theoretic consequences of the negative answer to Kirchberg’s embedding problem. In the unital $C^(*)$-algebra language, quantifier-free formulas are continuous combinations of norms of $*$-polynomials; variables range over prescribed norm balls. A unital algebra $E$ is #emph[existentially closed] if, for every unital inclusion $E subset.eq F$, every finite parameter tuple $a$ from $E$, and every quantifier-free formula $phi \( a \, y \)$ in a finite tuple $y$,

$ inf_(y in E) phi \( a \, y \) = inf_(y in F) phi \( a \, y \) \, $

where $y$ ranges over the corresponding products of prescribed norm balls. Thus an extension cannot improve the infimum of a finite quantifier-free test over $E$ #cite(label("GoldbringSinclair2015")) Section 2.1.

A parameter-free finite condition consists of finitely many strict inequalities $phi_j \( x \) < r_j$ with quantifier-free $phi_j$. It is #emph[satisfiable] when some tuple in a unital $C^(*)$-algebra satisfies them. The last assertion below makes the failure of good nuclear witnesses explicit as a positive lower bound on the approximation error of every finite-dimensional completely positive contractive factorization of every realizing tuple.

The KEP equivalences of Goldbring–Sinclair #cite(label("GoldbringSinclair2015")) Proposition 3.2 and Theorems 3.3 and 3.7 give these direct consequences of 1.

#thm([Corollary 6.1.])[
  (Consequences of negative KEP).  #emph[Neither $cal(O)_2$ nor any exact or nuclear unital $C^(*)$-algebra is existentially closed among unital $C^(*)$-algebra extensions.]
  #emph[For every free ultrafilter $omega$ on $bb(N)$, there are a separable unital $C^(*)$-algebra $D$ and $alpha in "Aut" \( D \)$ such that $D$ embeds unitally into $cal(O)_2^omega$ but $D times.r_alpha bb(Z)$ does not embed unitally there.]
  #emph[Some satisfiable finite condition $p \( x \) = { phi_j \( x \) < r_j : 1 lt.eq j lt.eq s }$ in the unital $C^(*)$-algebra language, with $x = \( x_1 \, dots.h \, x_m \)$, quantifier-free $phi_j$ and $r_j > 0$, lacks good nuclear witnesses: for some $epsilon_0 > 0$, every realization $a = \( a_i \)_(i = 1)^m$ of $p$ in every unital $C^(*)$-algebra $E$ satisfies $max_(1 lt.eq i lt.eq m) parallel psi phi.alt \( a_i \) - a_i parallel gt.eq epsilon_0$ for every finite-dimensional $C^(*)$-algebra $F$ and all completely positive contractive maps $phi.alt : E arrow.r F$, $psi : F arrow.r E$, not necessarily unital.]
] <cor:kep-consequences>

#proof[
  For each fixed free $omega$, 1 with $B = cal(O)_2$ negates unital KEP. The existential-closure claims follow from #cite(label("GoldbringSinclair2015")) Theorem 3.3, using that nuclearity implies exactness. Proposition 3.2 of the same paper, with its preceding construction for separable unital algebras, gives the crossed-product claim; Definition 3.6 and Theorem 3.7 give the finite-condition claim. These two witnesses are existential, not identified by our explicit group construction. ◻
]


